IRON BALL UPDATE: The possibility of dropping an item on a previous level while
falling down stairs has been eliminated. So be it.

In article <180@scorn.sco.COM> davidbe@sco.COM (The Cat in the Hat) writes:
>
>rec.games.hack's own kevin@cfctech.UUCP (Kevin Darcy) said:
>-...being punished really isn't as bad as it ought to be...
>
>Oh, I dunno...if I'm not playing a fighter type (say I'm playing a healer)
>then being punished causes me to dump a lot of useful stuff.  And I won't
>be able to wield the iron ball.
 
I don't entirely agree. Here is an inventory listing for a starting healer 
(strength = 8) after being punished and dropping some stuff: 

	Armor
	b - a +1 pair of leather gloves (being worn).
	Comestibles
	i - 8 uncursed apples.
	Potions
	e - 4 uncursed potions of extra healing.
	Wands
	f - a wand of sleep (8).
	Tools
	c - an uncursed stethoscope.
	Iron balls
	k - a heavy iron ball (chained to you) (weapon in hand).

Armor (such as it is), food, potions, the wand, the stethoscope, and an awesome
weapon. Missing are the spellbooks (chances are good that the healer could 
successfully study at least *one* of spells at first level), and 4 "normal" 
healing potions (which are often gone by the time the healer reaches Dlevel 2, 
in my experience). I would call this a viable character. If the punishment 
occurred a little later, there would probably be enough carrying capacity
freed up by the use of the extra healing potions, and possibly the wand, to 
accommodate some decent armor, food and/or extra weapon.

As far as similar classes go, tourists can always throw away extra food, 
priests are within one holy water of wielding the ball (they have spellbooks
they can read too), and rogues and archaeologists have sufficient strength
to pick up and wield the ball immediately. I think that covers all of the
non-fighter-type classes.

No-one ever said punishment was easy: I merely said it was more of an 
inconvenience than a disaster. Now one has to be careful when descending 
stairs. 

>Note: if you throw an iron ball (attached to you) you should automatically 
>trip.  If your strong enough, you should be dragged a bit.  In both cases 
>your leg should be injured (kicking wise) for a longer time than just a 
>muscle pull.

Interesting points here: 

	a) Throwing the ball while you are attached:

	   Physiologically, I think that it would be difficult to cause 
	   oneself serious damage by throwing an attached ball, since the 
	   range of throw would be very limited (relative to one's capacity to
	   absorb the effect of wrenching one's leg). The rules of the game 
	   permit a theoretical range limit of 5 for throwing an attached ball,
 	   which, if I understand it, is more than twice the length of the 
	   chain. If a change should be made, I would rather see the throwing 
	   range reduced.

	b) Should leg damage be caused by ball-dragging?

	   Probably. Will wait to see people's reaction (if any) to the new 
	   ball/stairs code. If they think it's already obnoxious, then leg 
	   damage will probably have to be shelved.

>And just what does punishment do to your relationship with your deity?

I just made a quick scan of the source. I can't see any place where punishment
affects your alignment or standing with your deity.
